@startuml alert-suppression
title US-03: Alert Suppression and Resume Handoff

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Alert\nEvaluator\n(leader)" as Evaluator
database "Redis\nalert-state:{id}" as AlertState
participant "Position\nConsumer" as Consumer
database "Redis\nentity:live:{id}" as LiveState
database "Redis\nrecent-loss:{id}" as RecentLoss

== Entity remains dark ==
loop scheduled scan
  Evaluator -> AlertState : HGETALL alert-state:ABC
  AlertState --> Evaluator : { dark_since_ms, signal_loss_alert_id, composite_issued }
  Evaluator -> Evaluator : active episode exists\ndo not create another SIGNAL_LOSS logical alert
end

== Entity resumes ==
Consumer -> LiveState : accept newer position for ABC
Consumer -> AlertState : HGETALL alert-state:ABC
AlertState --> Consumer : { dark_since_ms, signal_loss_alert_id, composite_issued }

alt prior signal-loss state exists
  Consumer -> RecentLoss : HSET recent-loss:ABC\ndark_since_ms\nresumed_at_ms = incoming.timestamp_ms\nsignal_loss_alert_id\nEXPIRE COMPOSITE_CORRELATION_WINDOW_MS
  Consumer -> AlertState : DEL alert-state:ABC
end

note over Consumer, RecentLoss
  recent-loss is written before alert-state deletion.
  This preserves a bounded post-resume correlation opportunity.
  The Alert Evaluator consumes/deletes it if a qualifying
  proximity candidate produces a COMPOSITE.
end note

note over AlertState
  alert-state is detection-loop/composite state only.
  Durable operator lifecycle state lives in TimescaleDB alerts.
end note

@enduml
